"The revelation of this Book is from Allah. It is emphasized that He possesses almighty power and wisdom. The attribute 'al-Aziz' signifies that Allah is never vanquished and has no partner. These two attributes highlight that the Quran is a mighty and wise book, and they prepare the way for the theme of tawhid (the oneness of God).\n\nPure religion belongs to Allah alone. Those who associate partners with Him claim that they worship idols, angels, or noble servants such as Jesus only so that 'they may bring us closer to Allah.' Yet this is baseless; worship must be rendered to Allah alone. Glad tidings are for those who avoid the Taghut (false deities) and turn to Allah.\n\nAllah has sent down the best of speech as a harmonious book consistent within itself and paired. The skins of those who hear it shiver, and then soften with the remembrance of Allah. This is Allah's guidance; He guides with it whom He wills, and whomever He leaves to stray, no one can guide.\n\nAllah presents a parable: A man belongs to many quarreling partners, while another belongs entirely to one person and is at peace. Can the two ever be equal? Praise belongs to Allah, yet most of them do not know. Both the Prophet and other people are mortal; on the Day of Resurrection, you will dispute in the presence of your Lord.\n\nThe righteous (muttaqun) who bring the truth and confirm it will have whatever they desire with their Lord. Allah will overlook even their worst deeds and reward them according to the best of what they did. Allah is sufficient for His servant; none other than Him should be feared.\n\nAllah takes the souls at the time of their death and during their sleep. He retains those for whom He has decreed death, and releases the others until a specified term. Indeed, in this are signs for those who reflect. In death and sleep, the soul is in Allah's hands; this points to the resurrection and the return to Allah.\n\nIntercession belongs entirely to Allah. No one can intercede without His permission. When Allah alone is mentioned, the hearts of the disbelievers shrink with aversion, but when others are mentioned, their faces brighten. O My servants! Do not despair of the mercy of Allah; indeed, He forgives all sins. Repent and turn to your Lord before the punishment comes.\n\nThe word 'yamin' means right hand, and it also conveys the meanings of strength and taking an oath. Here it can be interpreted as strength or an oath. As in the verse, \"On that Day We will roll up the sky like the rolling up of scrolls for writings\" (al-Anbiya, 21/104), a covenant has been made to roll up the sky. According to a narration from Aisha in Sahih Muslim, it was reported that during this time people will be upon the Sirat (the bridge).\n\nIn describing the Day of Resurrection, the blowing of the Trumpet (Sur) is recounted. Here there are two blowings: the first is the destructive 'nafkh al-sa'iq' (the blast of swooning), and the second is the raising 'nafkh al-qiyam' (the blast of resurrection). Although the word 'qiyamah' derives from the meaning of 'qiyam' (rising/standing) in the second blast, it also encompasses the first as its beginning; therefore, the breaking of the Hour signifies the greatest devastation.\n\nIn verses 69-70: Sa'iq refers to swooning and dying as if struck by a thunderbolt. Following this, the earth is made radiant. This is the gathering place of the Mahshar, which will be transformed in accordance with the expression in Surah Ibrahim, verse 48: \"On the Day the earth will be replaced with another earth.\" In a hadith, it is reported that people will be gathered upon a white ground. Regarding this place, which will be illuminated by the light of the Lord, Ibn Abbas stated that it is not the light of the sun and the moon, but another light that Allah will create. The Book (the record of deeds) is laid open, and the prophets and witnesses are brought forward.\n\nVerses 71-72: Those who disbelieved are driven into Hell in groups. Its gates are opened, and its keepers say to them: 'Did there not come to you messengers from among yourselves reciting to you My verses and warning you of the meeting of this Day of yours?' They say, 'Yes, they did.' But the decree of punishment has been realized against the disbelievers. It is said to them: 'Enter the gates of Hell to abide eternally therein.' How wretched is the abode of the arrogant!\n\nVerses 73-74: Those who feared their Lord are led to Paradise in groups. Its gates are opened, and its keepers say: 'Peace be upon you; you have done well! Enter it to abide eternally.' And they say: 'Praise be to Allah, who has fulfilled His promise to us and made us inherit the land of Paradise; we dwell wherever we please in Paradise.' How excellent is the reward of those who perform good deeds!\n\nVerse 75: You will see the angels surrounding the Throne, glorifying their Lord with praise. Judgment is executed among the people with truth, and it is proclaimed: 'Praise be to Allah, the Lord of the worlds.'"